.TH LIBMPQ__UPDATE_BEGIN 3 2026-09-25 "The MoPaQ archive library"
.SH NAME
libmpq__update_begin \- begin a filesystem MPQ update transaction
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <libmpq/mpq.h>
.sp
.BI "int32_t libmpq__update_begin(mpq_update_s **" "update" ", const char *" "path" ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Creates a same-directory private working copy of an existing regular MPQ or
container with an embedded MPQ at \fIpath\fP. The original file is unchanged
until commit. Embedded container prefixes and unrelated trailing bytes are
preserved; stale weak and strong signatures are removed on modification. On
failure, \fI*update\fP is NULL. MPQE archives require
\fBlibmpq__update_begin_mpqe\fP(3). The handle is consumed by commit or abort.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
V3/v4 mutations use available filenames and stored lookup identities.
Unrelated unnamed entries can be retained with fixed classic probe slots or
complete normalized 64-bit HET/BET hashes and consistent row provenance.
Classic hash capacity cannot grow without the missing probe-start hashes.
Unknown encrypted members retain their original offsets and packed bytes;
no filename key is guessed. Partial listfiles remain partial.
.PP
Reduced or ambiguous hashes, incompatible aliases, BET-only classic attributes,
embedded v3/v4 wrappers and patch members remain rejected without modifying
the staged archive. Rebuilds retain their version and regenerate metadata.
Invalidated signatures are removed. Unchanged commits and aborts require no
reconstruction. V4 preserves the source raw-chunk size, including zero, and
regenerates structural and raw MD5 digests. Retained packed representations
are preserved without recompression; known encrypted relocation adjusts keys.
.PP
Repeated names differing only by ASCII case or slash direction are accepted.
Ambiguous names, embedded null bytes and unresolvable listfile entries reject
reconstruction. A staged close failure prevents further mutation or publication;
abort remains safe and attempts cleanup.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns \fB0\fP on success, or a negative libmpq error.
.SH ERRORS
Invalid paths, unsupported formats, allocation, and backing I/O errors are
returned as negative LIBMPQ_ERROR_* values.
